---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { MARK, EDITION, SCORE_SHORT } from '../lib/brand';

/**
 * ONE LAYOUT, AND THAT IS A DECISION DAN MADE ON THE VIDEO.
 *
 * This page used to ship two: `?v=a` asked one question per screen, `?v=b` asked a section per
 * screen, and a switcher let a reviewer compare them. He compared them and chose: *"I like this
 * format better, because it's a lot of questions to do one at a time. It actually takes longer. So
 * I would do it by section."* The one-per-screen layout and the switcher went out rather than
 * being hidden behind a flag, because a flag would keep a dead branch alive in every function
 * below.
 *
 * WHAT REPLACED THE FLAT PROGRESS BAR. A section is no longer one screen: the real bank runs to
 * fourteen sections and eighty-odd questions, so a section is several pages, and Dan asked for both
 * levels to be visible. *"Maybe this shows like a slice, so maybe this has like three slices in
 * there and it highlights the one that you're on so that you can move to each one. And then you
 * move to the next section."* The rail below is those two levels: sections across the top, pages of
 * the current section beneath it. `steps()` in `lib/questions.ts` computes both.
 *
 * ── ONE RAIL, AND IT IS THE MARKS RAIL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The rail was itself reviewed as two drawings, behind a second `?v=` switch. One named all
 * fourteen sections under a segmented line; this one names ONE, at a size that can be read, with
 * the other thirteen as marks. The arithmetic is what settled it: fourteen sections across ~1296px
 * of content is about 92px a cell, and at 11px uppercase with 0.12em tracking that is roughly eight
 * characters a line — so every long title clamped to two lines and ended in an ellipsis.
 *
 * Dan chose the marks on 2026-08-28 and asked for three things, all of them below: the DIVIDED line
 * the other drawing had (every page its own tick, so the row reads as fourteen things and not as
 * one long bar), a tooltip naming a section on hover, and a visible difference between the sections
 * he can go back to and the ones he cannot reach yet. He has now asked for the labelled drawing to
 * go, and it has: there is one rail, one `paintProgress`, and no `?v=`. A stale `/survey?v=a` in
 * somebody's history is simply a survey URL with an unread parameter on it.
 */

/**
 * THE LOCK ON AN OUT-OF-REACH SECTION'S TOOLTIP.
 *
 * Dan, on the built rail: "maybe let's not write 'not yet available', and just put a padlock icon
 * instead." Lucide `lock`, inlined verbatim from `@iconify-json/lucide@1.2.114` and painted with
 * `currentColor` so one string serves both themes — the same treatment and the same package
 * version `CategoryExplainer.astro` uses for its three concept glyphs.
 *
 * IT IS DECORATIVE AND THE WORDS DID NOT GO ANYWHERE. A screen reader gets no icon, so
 * "not yet available" stays in the mark's own accessible name, exactly as it was
 * ("Change management, page 2 of 3, not yet available"). The whole tooltip is `aria-hidden`, so
 * the glyph is already outside the accessibility tree and there is nothing to announce twice.
 */
const ICON_LOCK =
  '<g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2"><rect width="18" height="11" x="3" y="11" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4"/></g>';
---

{/*
  THE FOOTER IS ON HERE NOW. It used to be suppressed on the grounds that attribution is noise on a
  screen where somebody is answering a question. Dan overruled that at 09:05: "I think this footer
  can stay there like this for the form, just not on the splash page." It sits under the flow, so a
  respondent reaches it only when they have stopped reading questions.
*/}
<Layout title={`Survey: ${EDITION}`} noindex>
  <main>
    <!-- Email-first start gate (survey-resume): email in, straight to question 1, no verification. -->
    <section id="gate" hidden>
      <h1>Before we start</h1>
      <p class="bo-lede">
        Where should we send your copy of the report? We'll also save your progress as you go, so
        you can leave and finish later.
      </p>
      <form id="gate-form" novalidate>
        <input
          type="email"
          id="gate-email"
          name="email"
          class="bo-field"
          placeholder="you@company.com"
          autocomplete="email"
        />
        <p class="bo-error-text" id="gate-error" hidden></p>
        <button type="submit" class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary">Get your {SCORE_SHORT}</button>
      </form>
      <p class="bo-fine">
        Already started on another device? <a href="/survey?resume=1">Get a resume link</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Cold-resume gate: magic-link request for expired sessions / new devices. -->
    <section id="resume-gate" hidden>
      <h1>Pick up where you left off</h1>
      <p class="bo-lede">
        Enter the email you started the survey with and we'll send you a sign-in link that opens
        your survey at your last question.
      </p>
      <form id="resume-form" novalidate>
        <input
          type="email"
          id="resume-email"
          name="email"
          class="bo-field"
          placeholder="you@company.com"
          autocomplete="email"
        />
        <p class="bo-error-text" id="resume-error" hidden></p>
        <button type="submit" class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary">Email me a resume link</button>
      </form>
      <p class="bo-fine">Starting fresh instead? <a href="/survey">Begin the survey</a></p>
    </section>

    <section id="resume-sent" hidden>
      <h1>Check your inbox</h1>
      <p class="bo-lede">
        We sent a resume link to <strong id="resume-sent-email"></strong>. Open it on this device
        to continue your survey. The link expires shortly, so use it soon.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The question flow -->
    <section id="quiz" hidden>
      <!-- THE MARK. Dan, 09:19: "all throughout, I think we should have like a state of DevOps dash
           Airtable, for kind of branding, that shows throughout." It is the middle of the meta row
           on every screen of the flow, and the same string appears on the splash and the finish
           page, from `lib/brand.ts`.

           THIS ROW WAS DELETED ON 2026-08-31 AND PUT BACK THE SAME DAY. Oleh took it out — *"it
           only distracts the user, let them focus purely on the survey and the progress bar"* —
           then reversed himself once the conflict was on the table: the strip is not ours to drop.
           Dan asked for it by name on the 09:19 review and asked to ADD the word "Survey" to it
           three days ago, so removing it would have undone a change he had just requested. Restored
           by reverting the commit, so what stands here is exactly what he asked for and not a
           reconstruction of it. Do not remove it again without him. -->
      <!-- `[Aggregate only]` USED TO CLOSE THIS ROW. Dan, 2026-08-28, clicking it: *"I don't know
           that we need that either."* It is not information lost, and it is worth being exact about
           where it survives, because an earlier version of this comment was not: the splash says
           "Aggregate only" in the fact row under the buttons, the credibility block ends on "Answers
           are confidential and only appear in aggregate", and the SITE FOOTER (`Layout.astro`)
           repeats that sentence on every page — this one included, while you answer. It is not said
           again at the gate; that claim was wrong and Oleh caught it.

           So the promise is made twice before question one and stays on screen throughout. Saying
           it a fourth time in the meta row made it decoration rather than a term. The row is a
           two-part meta line now: which question you are on, and what you are filling in. -->
      <div class="q-topbar q-topbar--pair">
        <span id="q-tag">[S1]</span>
        <span class="q-topbar__mid">{MARK}</span>
      </div>

      <!--
        ONE RAIL, AND THE SECTION'S OWN RULE CARRIES THE PAGES.
        The first build drew a second rail of short bars under the sections, which is what Dan
        sketched. Oleh's reading on 2026-08-27 is better and it is the one built: the line above a
        section IS the section, so it divides into one part per page rather than a separate row
        appearing underneath it. A section with one page keeps one unbroken rule and nothing about
        the row moves; a section with three is visibly in thirds while you are inside it.
      -->
      <div class="q-progress">
        <!-- ONE THING ON THIS LINE. "Some answers add a step. The count grows when they do."
             stood at the right end until 2026-08-28, when Dan selected it and said *"I think we
             could remove this, I don't think that adds anything."* He is right about what it was
             doing: it explained a mechanism nobody had asked about yet, in the smallest type on
             the screen, above the question. The rail itself already tells the truth — a mark
             divides into more parts when a branch adds a page, and it does that while you watch. -->
        <div class="q-progress__head">
          <span class="q-progress__count" id="step-count">Section 1</span>
        </div>
        <!--
          `role="list"`, NOT `role="progressbar"`, AND THAT IS A BUG FIX RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
          `progressbar` is one of the roles ARIA declares "children presentational", so every mark
          inside this element was being pruned out of the accessibility tree — all fourteen
          sections, invisible to a screen reader no matter what `aria-label` each one carried.
          Nothing announced them and nothing could reach them.
          `list-style: none` on this `<ol>` also drops list semantics in Safari, so the role is
          written out rather than left implicit. The progress itself is not lost: `Section 5 of 14`
          is real text in the row above, which is where a reader was getting it anyway.
        -->
        <ol class="q-progress__track" id="section-track" role="list" aria-label="Survey sections"
        ></ol>

        <!--
          THE TOOLTIP, AND THERE IS EXACTLY ONE OF IT.
          Dan on the review call: "you can go back, but you can't remember which one you're going
          back to. Maybe it's a hover or something." One shared node rather than fourteen, because
          the thing that has to be got right is the CLAMPING at the two ends of the row — the first
          and the fourteenth section both want to hang off the edge of the viewport — and that is
          one piece of arithmetic done once, against the rail's own box.

          It lives in the TEMPLATE, not in `paintProgress`, so it carries Astro's scope attribute
          and its styles can sit in the scoped block with the rest of the row's placement. Every
          part of the rail that `paintProgress` builds still has to be styled globally.

          `aria-hidden` because it is a second rendering of something already in the accessible
          name of the mark that summons it: the mark says "Incidents, not yet available" whether it
          is hovered, focused or read. Duplicating that into a live region would say it twice.
        -->
        <div class="q-tip" id="marks-tip" aria-hidden="true" data-show="false" data-reach="true">
          <span class="q-tip__state" id="marks-tip-state" hidden>
            <svg class="q-tip__lock" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" set:html={ICON_LOCK} />
          </span>
          <span class="q-tip__name" id="marks-tip-name"></span>
        </div>

        <!--
          THE SECOND LINE. The marks name nothing on their own, so the current section's title is
          written once underneath them, at a size that can be read — which is the whole trade the
          marks make, and the reason they beat fourteen clamped labels. `Page N of M` and `Next ·`
          are the rest of it, handed back in words rather than in cells.
        -->
        <div class="q-marks__line" id="marks-line">
          <!--
            `tabindex="-1"` SO A PAGE CHANGE CAN LAND HERE. Dan: "every new page should start from
            the top as the user goes through the survey." Scrolling alone only fixes it for a
            mouse — a keyboard or screen-reader user pressing Next is left on the old page's Next
            button — so `toPageTop()` moves focus here as well, and this is the element it moves it
            to: it is the topmost thing on the screen that says where you now are, and after the
            compact header went it is the ONLY place the section is named on pages two and up.
            Not focusable by Tab, only by script; the ring is left to `survey.css`'s
            `:focus-visible` rule, which paints it after Enter on Next and not after a click.
          -->
          <h2 class="q-marks__name" id="marks-name" tabindex="-1"></h2>
          <span class="q-marks__page" id="marks-page" hidden></span>
          <span class="q-marks__next" id="marks-next" hidden></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!--
        THE SECTION HEADER, AND IT CHANGES SIZE ON PURPOSE.
        Dan: "I'd make this more prominent, the section that you're in. Maybe it's more of a header
        here, you can see it there but this is just kind of small. So maybe the description is up
        above." And then: "maybe the first page has more of a header about what section you're on,
        and once you get to the next one it moves to this, to kind of give some real estate back."
        So page one of a section gets the title, the blurb and any intro; pages two and up keep the
        title on one compact line and drop the rest. `data-lead` on the element is the switch.
      -->
      <header class="q-section" id="q-section" data-lead="true">
        <p class="q-section__eyebrow" id="q-section-eyebrow"></p>
        <h1 class="q-section__title" id="q-section-title"></h1>
        <p class="q-section__blurb" id="q-section-blurb"></p>
        <aside class="q-note" id="q-section-intro" hidden>
          <p class="q-note__label">Note</p>
          <p class="q-note__body" id="q-section-intro-text"></p>
        </aside>
      </header>

      <div class="q-body">
        <form id="step" novalidate></form>
      </div>

      <div class="q-footbar">
        <button type="button" class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost" id="back" hidden>Back</button>
        <div class="q-footbar__end">
          <p class="bo-save" id="save-state" data-state="idle" aria-live="polite">
            <span class="bo-save__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span id="save-label">Progress saves as you go</span>
          </p>
          <button type="button" class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" id="next">Next</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* PAGE PLACEMENT ONLY. Colour, radius and type come from `survey.css` tokens and recipes. */

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-32);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  #gate form,
  #resume-form {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    width: 100%;
  }

  #gate,
  #resume-gate,
  #resume-sent {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    margin-block: auto;
    padding-block: var(--bo-space-5);
  }

  #gate > .bo-fine,
  #resume-gate > .bo-fine {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  /* ── THE QUIZ SHELL ─ the rail at the top, the question breathing, the nav at the bottom ───── */
  #quiz {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;

    /* ONE NUMBER FOR THE QUESTION COLUMN, and it is here because THREE blocks have to agree on it:
       the section header, the questions, and the nav bar under them. It was written out three times
       and about to be a fourth, which is how a column and the rule under it come to disagree by a
       rem and nobody can say when. */
    --q-col: 42rem;
  }

  .q-topbar {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── TWO ITEMS, AND THE MARK STILL SITS ON THE PAGE'S AXIS ─────────────────────────────────
     `[Aggregate only]` closed this row until 2026-08-28 and was doing invisible layout work:
     `space-between` across three items is what put the mark in the middle of the page. With two,
     the same rule centres the mark inside whatever is left of the row after `[S1]`, which lands it
     visibly right of centre — and the mark is the one thing on this line that has to look
     deliberate, since it is the branding Dan asked to run throughout.

     A three-track grid with equal outer columns centres it on the page axis instead, and does it
     from the container rather than from the contents: the third track is empty and stays that way,
     so the mark holds its position no matter how long `[S4.1]` gets. The two children auto-place
     into tracks 1 and 2.

     It overrides `display` and `justify-content` from `.q-topbar` at equal specificity, so it has
     to stay BELOW that rule in this file. */
  .q-topbar--pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
  }

  .q-topbar__mid {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* `position: relative` is what the tooltip is measured and placed against. It has to be THIS
     box and not the track: the track is the row of marks, and a tooltip clamped to the track's
     width is clamped to exactly the width it needs to stay inside. */
  .q-progress {
    position: relative;
    padding-top: var(--bo-space-2);
  }

  /* ── THE TOOLTIP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Absolutely positioned, so it costs the page NO vertical space and cannot move the rhythm of
     the row it hangs under. `top` and the horizontal `translate` are both written by the script
     from real measurements; everything that does not depend on which mark is under the pointer is
     here.

     `max-width` is the whole overflow story. The rail is as wide as the content column, so a
     tooltip that can never exceed 100% of `.q-progress` can never reach past the column, and the
     script clamps its left edge into the same box. Fourteen sections means the first and the
     fourteenth tooltip both want to hang off an edge; neither can. */
  .q-tip {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    /* ONE ROW. The lock sat on its own line under the name and read as a second, unexplained
       statement rather than as a property of the one above it — Oleh, 2026-08-28. Leading the
       name, it is what a status glyph normally is: a mark ON the label. `flex-start` rather than
       `center` because the name wraps at 22rem and centring a glyph against a two-line block
       floats it between the lines; the name's line box is 18.9px against a 16px lock, so aligned
       tops already read as one line. */
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.375rem;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    max-width: min(22rem, 100%);
    padding: 0.375rem 0.625rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition:
      opacity 0.14s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
      visibility 0.14s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
  }

  .q-tip[data-show='true'] {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* BLUISH WHERE HE CAN GO, GREYISH WHERE HE CANNOT — Dan's sketch, in tokens. The border does
     most of the work because the surface under both is the same raised grey; the name's colour
     seconds it, and the second line says the reason in words for anyone who does not read the
     difference as a difference. */
  .q-tip[data-reach='true'] {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .q-tip__name {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* THE FULL NAME, FINALLY. "Documentation and schema discipline" clamped to two lines and an
     ellipsis in an 82px labelled cell, which is what the marks were chosen over; in here it has
     22rem and wraps. */
  .q-tip[data-reach='true'] .q-tip__name {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* ── THE LOCK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A line of spaced caps became roughly one glyph's worth of ink, so the two things that keep it
     legible are size and weight, not decoration: 18px on a 24 grid at Lucide's own 2px stroke, and
     `--bo-muted-strong` rather than `--bo-muted`, which is the difference between a lock you read
     and a lock you notice afterwards.

     IT IS NOT CARRYING THE DISTINCTION ALONE and that is why one glyph is enough here. The
     unreachable tooltip already differs in two other places at the same moment — a neutral border
     where the reachable one is accent, and a muted name where the reachable one is accent — and
     the mark under the pointer has lifted to grey rather than to accent. The lock is the third
     signal and the one that says WHY; it was never the only one. */
  .q-tip__state {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Matches the name's line box, so the glyph centres on the first line rather than on its own
       16px. */
    height: 1.35em;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  .q-tip__lock {
    /* 18px was sized to replace a whole line of spaced caps and carry the meaning on its own.
       Beside the name it no longer has to: the words are back, so the glyph steps down to sit
       with 14px text rather than loom over it. `flex-shrink` because a long section name must
       never squeeze it. */
    width: 16px;
    height: 16px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* The caret is a rotated square, and its horizontal position is the ONE thing that survives the
     clamp: when the tooltip is pushed back inside the column its body no longer sits over the mark
     that summoned it, so the caret is what still points at it. */
  .q-tip::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: -4px;
    left: var(--tip-caret, 50%);
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    margin-left: -3.5px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    transform: rotate(45deg);
  }

  .q-tip[data-reach='true']::before {
    border-top-color: var(--bo-primary);
    border-left-color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* `survey.css` already collapses every transition to 0.01ms under this preference, which is the
     blunt global reset. This is the same intent written where the transition is, so the next person
     to read these rules does not have to know the reset exists to know the fade is optional. The
     tooltip still appears and disappears — only the fading stops. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .q-tip {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .q-progress__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--bo-space-3);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .q-progress__count {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-12);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The row's own `gap` is not here: it is `--mark-gap`, set with the marks further down, because
     the two values it takes are a measurement about the marks and belong beside them. */
  .q-progress__track {
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-flow: column;
    grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* ── THE MARKS RAIL'S LINE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One row, baseline-aligned: the section's name, its page position, and what is coming, with the
     space between the second and the third doing the separating so no rule is needed. */
  .q-marks__line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .q-marks__name {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .q-marks__page,
  .q-marks__next {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* `Next ·` is pushed to the far end and is the first thing to go when the row runs out of room:
     it is the least load-bearing of the three, and a wrapped micro-label reads as a fault. */
  .q-marks__next {
    margin-left: auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  /* ── THE SECTION HEADER ─ the blurb and the note on page one, nothing on the pages after ───── */
  .q-section {
    /* `--bo-space-3` and not `--bo-space-5`: the rail's own line already names the section 40px
       higher, so the header underneath it opens the blurb rather than announcing a new screen. */
    padding-top: var(--bo-space-3);
  }

  .q-section__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-1);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .q-section__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-32);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .q-section__blurb {
    margin: var(--bo-space-2) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--q-col);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* THE SECTION INTRO IS A NOTE, AND IT IS BUILT LIKE ONE.
     It is not a caption: bank section 7 defines what "structural change" means for the ten
     questions under it, and section P promises those answers are never published. Set as grey text
